      <mri:abstract>
        <gco:CharacterString>NZLUM (New Zealand Land Use and Management classification) is a national land use and management dataset for New Zealand, produced by combinatorial classification of many authoritative spatial datasets aligned to a discrete global grid system (DGGS), using the Land Use Information System (LUIS). Each location is independently assessed against a three-tier hierarchy of land use classes (primary/secondary/tertiary codes) defined by the NZSLUC classification system: 1 = Conservation and natural environments (e.g. nature conservation, cultural and natural heritage, minimal-use natural environments including defence land, unused/transitioning land); 2 = Production land uses (e.g. plantation forestry, dairy and dry stock grazing, cropping, horticulture, intensive animal production, water/wastewater, smallholder farms); 3 = Built environments (e.g. residential density tiers, recreation and community services, commercial, manufacturing and industrial, utilities, transportation, mining, waste treatment). Alongside the class identification, each unit carries a confidence score, spatial scale, and source date range describing the evidentiary basis of its classification.</gco:CharacterString>
      </mri:abstract>
      <mri:purpose>
        <gco:CharacterString>To provide a single, nationally consistent land use and management classification for New Zealand's territorial sea extent, resolving many overlapping and variably-scaled authoritative datasets (national topographic, cadastral, land-cover, and land-use datasets; and regional council resource-consent, hazard, and land-use datasets) into one dataset with a common schema, suitable for national-scale land use analysis, reporting, and planning.</gco:CharacterString>
      </mri:purpose>
